In 2024,<!--DO NOT CHANGE WITHOUT ALSO UPDATING LEAD AT TOP OF ARTICLE!--> world wheat production was 799<!--DO NOT CHANGE WITHOUT ALSO UPDATING LEAD AT TOP OF ARTICLE!--> million tonnes, led by China, India, and Russia which collectively provided 42% of the world total (table). 

In 2024, [[List of countries by wheat exports|the largest exporters were]] Russia (32.7 million tonnes), Canada (25.5), United States (21.8), and Ukraine (20.7),<ref>{{cite web |publisher=UN Food and Agriculture Organization, Statistics Division, FAOSTAT |title=Top 10 Countries, Export Quantity of Wheat, Rankings |url=https://www.fao.org/faostat/en/#rankings/countries_by_commodity_exports |date=2026 |access-date=19 April 2026}}</ref><!--Do not archive this dated source - it changes yearly, as do the table data--> while the largest importers were Egypt (13.1 million tonnes), Indonesia (12.1), and China (11.0).<ref>{{cite web |publisher=UN Food and Agriculture Organization, Statistics Division, FAOSTAT |title=Top 10 Countries, Import Quantity of Wheat, Rankings |url=https://www.fao.org/faostat/en/#rankings/countries_by_commodity_exports |date=2026 |access-date=19 April 2026}}</ref><!--Do not archive this dated source - it changes yearly, as do the table data-->

In 2024, wheat was grown on {{convert|219.5|e6ha|abbr=off|disp=or}} worldwide, more than any other food crop.<ref>{{cite web |title=Wheat area harvested, world total from pick lists: Crops/World regions/Area harvested/Year|publisher=UN Food and Agriculture Organization, Statistics Division, FAOSTAT |url=https://www.fao.org/faostat/en/#data/QCL |date=2026 |access-date=19 April 2026 }}</ref><!--Do not archive this dated source - it changes yearly, as do the table data--> 
World trade in wheat at the end of the 20th century was greater than for all other crops combined.<ref>{{cite web |last=Curtis |first=B. C. |title=Wheat in the World |url=https://www.fao.org/4/y4011e/y4011e04.htm |publisher=[[Food and Agriculture Organization]] |access-date=10 June 2026}}</ref>

Global demand for wheat is increasing due to the unique [[viscoelastic]] and adhesive properties of [[gluten]] proteins, which facilitate the production of processed foods, whose consumption is increasing as a result of the worldwide industrialisation process and [[Western pattern diet|westernisation of diets]].<ref name="Shewry-2015" /><ref name="Day-2006">{{cite journal |last1=Day |first1=L. |last2=Augustin |first2=M.A. |last3=Batey |first3=I.L. |last4=Wrigley |first4=C.W. |title=Wheat-gluten uses and industry needs |journal=[[Trends in Food Science & Technology]] |publisher=Elsevier |volume=17 |issue=2 |pages=82–90 |date=2006 |type=Review |doi=10.1016/j.tifs.2005.10.003 }}</ref>

